# B4l — AN OCTAGONAL TOWER, AND THE PALETTE IS WHAT SAYS SO
#
# ⛔ **45° IS NOT A LATTICE DIRECTION AT ALL.** `tan 45° = 1` needs `m/k = √3`, which
# is irrational, so no integer `(k, m)` gives it at any length — an octagonal face can
# never be a `D` wall, which is why an octagon is its own BODY in the palette rather
# than another heading (BLUEPRINT §2.3).
#
# ⚠ **AND IT NEEDS NO VERB OF ITS OWN.** `B4i` measured the body shut for `verb wall`
# — *a body says a wall is round; it cannot say how big* — and `verb tower` is the one
# gesture that already HAS a size. So the shape comes from the type and the size from
# `select shell`, each through its one reader.
#
# ⚠ **SHELL 468 IS CHOSEN, NOT CONVENIENT.** At seven shells below it — 12, 48, 144,
# 192, 300, 432, 444 — a regular octagon quantises back onto the DISK and the two
# bodies build one world; the gesture refuses those. 468 is the first shell above all
# of them, and the first that reads as an octagon in plan.

# ⚠ `verb raise` PUTS ITS HILL TEN HEXES AHEAD, NOT UNDERFOOT — `raise_ahead`'s own
# rule, and the plan view is where that becomes visible: the 91-cell disc this line
# leaves sits east of both towers rather than under either. It is here for parity with
# `tower.keys` and to give the picture something with a material in it; the rims do not
# need it, because a wall byte is stored whether or not the cell under it has ground.
echo --- a hill, ten hexes east of where the author is standing
at 0 0 0
verb raise

echo --- two wall types, one round and one octagonal
declare edge 5 curtain body=THICK_CURVED thick=0.5
declare edge 6 octtower body=THICK_OCT thick=0.5

echo --- the octagon
select wall 6
select shell 468
at 0 0 0
verb tower

echo --- and the circle of the same shell beside it, from the same verb
select wall 5
at 40 0 0
verb tower
